WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:07.674
[upbeat pizzicato music]

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.177
Aharoni: My name is Tomer.

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:12.221
I'm a computer science student.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:13.639
I like to see

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.475
people enjoy the things I make.

00:00:16.517 --> 00:00:18.352
One day, I got a voice note.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:20.395
I wanted to listen to it instantly,

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:23.440
but I didn't want to play it
in the middle of the class.

00:00:23.482 --> 00:00:25.150
So, using Google Docs,

00:00:25.192 --> 00:00:28.904
I was able to dictate
that voice note into text.

00:00:28.946 --> 00:00:31.865
Then I started thinking,
can I do something bigger than that?

00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:32.950
And I was like,

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:34.117
"Hey, Alon, what's up?

00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:36.537
"Do you want to maybe create phone calls

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:39.873
for people who are deaf
or hard of hearing?"

00:00:39.915 --> 00:00:41.542
Ezer: The common way to communicate

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:43.418
with someone who is deaf
or hard of hearing

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.837
is using a relay service.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.590
You can't use the phone
without someone listening to you.

00:00:48.632 --> 00:00:50.133
Aharoni: And we wanted to fix that.

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:52.719
Ezer: The hardest part
was not the technology

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:55.973
but understanding,
how are we gonna build it?

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:57.641
When we encountered Google Cloud,

00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:59.601
it changed everything for us.

00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:02.104
They had the text-to-speech APIs
that we needed.

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:05.649
They had hosting services
and everything under one roof.

00:01:05.691 --> 00:01:07.025
Aharoni: I think in less than 12 hours,

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.654
we were able to create a proof of concept.

00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:13.532
The moment we saw Nagish working
was the moment

00:01:13.574 --> 00:01:15.826
I felt like an actual software engineer,

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:17.995
because I created something.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:19.413
We tested it ourselves,

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:22.082
but we didn't get to actually test it
with real users.

00:01:22.124 --> 00:01:25.085
Today, we hope to do that
for the first time.

00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:27.045
[uplifting music]

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.046
Taliah: Hi.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:29.923
Aharoni: How are you today?
Taliah: I'm good.

00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:31.425
Aharoni: Yeah?
Taliah: My name is Taliah.

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:34.219
I'm 18 years old,
and I'm from New York City.

00:01:34.261 --> 00:01:35.554
I am hard of hearing.

00:01:35.596 --> 00:01:38.348
So I read lips
in order to understand better.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.392
When, like, I speak to someone

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:42.895
that doesn't know
that I have hearing loss,

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.314
they sometime, like, mock me as a joke.

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:49.151
It makes me, like,
just feel bad about myself.

00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:50.527
Aharoni: Do you use phone calls?

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:51.778
Do you have smartphone?

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.863
What do you do today?

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:54.406
Taliah: No, I don't use the phone.

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:56.909
I just decline it if I understand
what they're trying to say,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.994
and then I text them,
"Oh, what do you say?"

00:01:59.036 --> 00:02:00.245
Aharoni: Yeah.
Taliah: "I didn't hear you."

00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:01.622
Aharoni: We want to solve that.
Taliah: Okay.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:03.540
The first person that I would call

00:02:03.582 --> 00:02:05.125
is my mom.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.543
My mom's my role model.

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:10.547
Aharoni: Maybe you could call her
and see how it works.

00:02:10.589 --> 00:02:11.840
[phone line ringing]

00:02:11.882 --> 00:02:14.134
automated voice: This is
an accessible phone call.

00:02:14.176 --> 00:02:15.177
[phone beeps]

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:19.515
You are now connected.

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:20.557
Janet: Hi, Taliah.

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:22.559
What are you doing?

00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:26.146
Taliah: Oh, wow.

00:02:26.188 --> 00:02:27.731
[laughter]

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:30.901
Janet: Hey, are you going to go out
with your friends today?

00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:33.654
automated voice: Yes, I am.

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:35.364
[laughter]

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.908
Janet: To see her smiling makes me smile.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.285
I think this is a wonderful thing for--

00:02:40.327 --> 00:02:42.829
for, you know, kids that are struggling.

00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:44.873
This would be, like,
a different world to them.

00:02:44.915 --> 00:02:47.167
Aharoni: Yeah.
It makes me super happy.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:49.419
Like, how was it for you

00:02:49.461 --> 00:02:50.587
to place a phone call like that?

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:51.839
Taliah: Honestly, it makes me feel like

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.174
I can do the stuff
that normal people can do.

00:02:54.216 --> 00:02:55.759
It's amazing feeling.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:57.678
Yeah.

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:03.725
[uplifting music]

